### Subscribe to Family Office Insights [...] Single-Family Office: A single-family office operates privately and independently, serving only one family. This type of office provides highly personalized and exclusive support, managing all aspects of the family’s financial and personal affairs. Due to their high level of personalization and comprehensive approach, they tend to be quite costly.
 Multi-Family Office: A multi-family office offers comprehensive wealth management services to multiple families. By pooling resources and expertise, MFOs offer cost-effective solutions while delivering a high standard of service. Each family benefits from shared administrative and investment management services.",What is a Family Office? A Complete Family Office Guide,,,2026-07-28T09:59:10.209757,Comprehensive Family Office Wealth Services: An In-Depth Exploration,,Validation score: 6,Verified,,,EXISTING,,,,"Explore family offices, their purpose, services, and types. Learn how a family office can help you manage wealth and support your high-net-worth family.",Tavily,,[],"What is a Family Office? A Complete Family Office Guide Skip to content Search for: Login Family Office Guide Knowledge Base Subscribe to Family Office Insights Email Subscribe By providing your email address, you consent to receive messages from Cresset regarding our services. View our Privacy Policy . Related Topics What Is a Multi-Family Office? Family Office Services What Is a Family Office? A Complete Family Office Guide 23 minute read A s a high-net-worth individual, you have likely encountered the term “family office” with increasing frequency. This trend reflects the growing popularity of family offices as a preferred option for managing substantial wealth. But what exactly is a family office? In this comprehensive guide, we will thoroughly explore the concept, clarifying what family offices are, the types of family offices that exist, their target clientele, and what they do. Though family offices are experiencing a surge in popularity, conceptually they are not new: in fact, family offices date back to 15th century Italy. Over time, they have developed into our modern conception of a family office, which provides comprehensive, personalized wealth management strategies to high-net-worth and ultra-high-net-worth families. Consider this your definitive family office guide – a centralized resource providing all of the information you need to understand the ever-evolving nature of family offices . We aim to equip you with the knowledge to assess whether a family office aligns with your family’s financial objectives, and how one could help in preserving and expanding your wealth for future generations. Request an Introduction Family Office Definition: What Is a Family Office? To start, let’s define what a family office is. In essence, a family wealth office is a private firm that manages wealth and investments for a family or group of families. However, that definition is a bit simplistic for what a family office actually does. While early family offices did begin with a focus on managing the taxes, investments, and philanthropic endeavors of wealthy families, over time their offerings and approach have expanded what wealth management means for families, and now includes concierge services, estate planning, family governance and education, wealth preservation, and more. In brief, family office wealth management is holistic, with a focus not only on growing wealth, but on the seamless transfer of wealth across generations. These offices provide a consolidated approach to managing family wealth, integrating investment management, financial planning, estate planning, and more. As we delve deeper, we will explore the different types of family offices, and the extensive offerings they provide. What Does a Family Office Do? Family offices have the unique capability to customize their strategies to the specific needs and goals of the families they serve. Family offices are comprehensive and multifaceted, addressing both strategic financial planning and daily financial management tasks for clients. Their offerings typically include: Estate and Succession Planning Goals-Based Financial Planning and Investing Tax Planning and Compliance Philanthropic Advisory Family Governance and Education Lifestyle Services The list above is non-comprehensive, and we will cover all of these offerings and more in greater detail below. While these solutions may seem wide-reaching, by providing all-encompassing support, family offices ensure that the financial and personal needs of the family are met. This approach acknowledges that financial planning, investment strategies, and lifestyle are interconnected, allowing for both the preservation and growth of wealth across generations. Common Family Office Offerings Here, we will more closely examine the most commonly provided aspects of a family office, including those mentioned above. You will notice that in addition to traditional wealth management solutions, family offices also handle seemingly non-financial issues, such as family education and lifestyle services. Goals-Based Planning & Investing Because family offices utilize an integrated and personalized approach, they are well-equipped to structure both financial planning and investment strategies with specific goals in mind. Both financial planning strategies and investment portfolios will be focused on aligning with the family’s overall financial objectives. Family office investing as a practice is as varied as family offices themselves since each family’s short- and long-term goals will be distinct. However, there tends to be a focus on long-term growth, with an emphasis on growing wealth across generations. Family offices also tend to provide a unique level of control regarding how and where investments are made. Each part of the investment process will be personalized, from determining goals to assessing the family’s level of risk tolerance. From there, a diversified investment portfolio will be constructed to meet the defined goals. These customized portfolios will often contain a combination of asset classes, including equities, fixed income, real estate, and alternative investments. Family offices may also offer direct access to private market investment opportunities. These private investments offer the opportunity for higher returns and portfolio diversification. They may also offer greater control and influence compared to public investments, as well as a more long-term focus. Specific investment strategies will vary based on each family’s unique finances but may include first defining investment goals, diversifying portfolios and income-producing assets, and various strategies to maximize returns over time. Similarly, financial planning will begin with identifying the family’s unique objectives. From there, a comprehensive financial strategy will be developed based on the family’s identified goals and current financial situation. The client will be involved in the monitoring process, and adjustments can be made as goals and finances change over time. Strategic Tax Planning A comprehensive understanding of the family’s financial situation and future goals enables family offices to create and implement a proactive tax plan that minimizes tax liabilities and ensures compliance with tax laws. Family offices stay up-to-date on current tax laws and regulations so that the family does not need to, which means they will maintain accurate and effective tax planning strategies over many years. Strategic tax planning is intrinsically linked with investment planning, as tax-efficient investment strategies are a crucial component of mitigating tax impact. Tax planning intersects with multiple aspects of a family office, involving not only investment restructuring, but also establishing trusts, adjusting business structures, and retirement planning. Each family’s unique goals and financial circumstances shape the approach to strategic tax planning. Family offices may employ a range of techniques, from traditional methods to cutting-edge advancements such as AI tax preparation . Regardless of the methods used, a successful strategy will always prioritize fulfilling the family’s specific objectives. Wealth Strategy Wealth strategists develop comprehensive wealth plans to ensure smooth and effective generational wealth transfer. These strategies involve optimizing both lifetime gifts and inheritances to ensure that wealth is transferred efficiently with minimal tax implications. Wealth strategies also include asset protection, lifestyle funding, cash flow analysis, and more. A family office will tailor their strategies to the needs and goals of the family to preserve and grow wealth across generations. A well-crafted wealth strategy not only secures family legacy, it supports the family’s overall stability and prosperity. Working closely with clients, a family office wealth strategist will provide tailored solutions that align with their long-term objectives, so that wea",
