be966812-dab6-426d-b27d-e4247c35260f,Private equity and sports: A natural partnership,https://handler.law/the-family-office-playbook-sports-as-a-diversification-and-legacy-strategy,VERIFIED_FAMILY_OFFICE,0.95,Verified via explicit name cross-matching and signature patterns.,6,,,"The trend reflects a broader shift in family office investing. As families look beyond traditional portfolios of public securities, private equity and real estate, many are seeking direct investments that offer greater control, visibility and alignment with long-term family objectives. Sports investments increasingly fit that profile. 

Several factors are driving interest. Professional sports franchises and leagues benefit from a level of scarcity rarely found in other asset classes. There are only so many teams, leagues and premier sports properties available for investment, creating natural barriers to entry and contributing to significant valuation growth over time. [...] Importantly, family offices are not limiting their attention to franchise ownership. Investors are increasingly exploring opportunities in women’s sports, sports technology companies, emerging leagues and related businesses serving the sports ecosystem. These investments can provide exposure to the industry’s growth without requiring ownership of a major professional team. 

For many families, sports investments offer benefits beyond financial returns. Unlike many alternative assets, sports properties often create opportunities for multi-generational engagement, community involvement and legacy building. A sports-related investment can become part of a family’s identity while also serving broader wealth preservation and growth objectives.","The Family Office Playbook: Sports as a Diversification and Legacy Strategy - Handler Law, LLP - Handler Law, LLP",,,2026-07-28T10:01:26.831994,Private equity and sports: A natural partnership,,Validation score: 6,Verified,,,EXISTING,,,,,Tavily,,['info@handler.law'],"The Family Office Playbook: Sports as a Diversification and Legacy Strategy - Handler Law, LLP - Handler Law, LLP ← Return to News The Family Office Playbook: Sports as a Diversification and Legacy Strategy As family offices continue to pursue alternative investments, sports assets are attracting attention for their scarcity, growth potential and legacy-building characteristics. Professional sports ownership was once limited to a small group of billionaire families and media companies. Today, however, sports is increasingly being viewed as a legitimate alternative asset class, attracting growing interest from family offices seeking diversification, long-term growth and unique investment opportunities. The trend reflects a broader shift in family office investing. As families look beyond traditional portfolios of public securities, private equity and real estate, many are seeking direct investments that offer greater control, visibility and alignment with long-term family objectives. Sports investments increasingly fit that profile. Several factors are driving interest. Professional sports franchises and leagues benefit from a level of scarcity rarely found in other asset classes. There are only so many teams, leagues and premier sports properties available for investment, creating natural barriers to entry and contributing to significant valuation growth over time. At the same time, the economics of sports have evolved considerably. Teams and leagues are no longer dependent solely on ticket sales and local sponsorships. Revenue streams now include media rights, streaming agreements, global sponsorships, licensing opportunities, sports betting partnerships and real estate developments surrounding venues. As sports organizations continue to expand their global reach, many investors view the sector as offering long-term growth potential supported by multiple sources of revenue. Importantly, family offices are not limiting their attention to franchise ownership. Investors are increasingly exploring opportunities in women’s sports, sports technology companies, emerging leagues and related businesses serving the sports ecosystem. These investments can provide exposure to the industry’s growth without requiring ownership of a major professional team. For many families, sports investments offer benefits beyond financial returns. Unlike many alternative assets, sports properties often create opportunities for multi-generational engagement, community involvement and legacy building. A sports-related investment can become part of a family’s identity while also serving broader wealth preservation and growth objectives. Like any significant investment, sports assets require careful planning and diligence. Ownership structures, governance requirements, tax considerations, liquidity constraints and succession planning issues should all be evaluated before making an investment. As interest in the sector continues to grow, families should ensure that sports investments are considered within the context of an overall wealth, governance and estate planning strategy. While sports may once have been viewed primarily as a passion investment, family offices are increasingly recognizing the industry as a sophisticated asset class with meaningful opportunities for growth, diversification and legacy building. From family office structuring and tax planning to governance and succession strategies, sports investments often involve considerations that extend well beyond the initial transaction. Handler Law advises families and family offices on the legal and planning issues that accompany complex and multi-generational investments. To discuss a sports-related investment opportunity or family office planning strategy, contact us. ← Previous Share on LinkedIn Share on X Email Copy Link Print Next → Contact Us - Handler Law, LLP - Handler Law, LLP Contact Us Phone (312) 641-2100 Email info@handler.law LinkedIn @handlerlaw-llp Address 191 North Wacker Drive, Ste. 2300 Chicago, IL 60606-1633 Visiting our Office Airports Handler Law is conveniently located near two major airports. Chicago O’Hare International Airport is approximately 15 miles northwest of our office, while Chicago Midway International Airport is about 10 miles to the southwest. Public Transit & Commuter Rail Our office is easily accessible via public transportation. The CTA Green Line’s Clinton stop is just one block away. The Clark/Lake station—serving the Brown, Green, Orange, Pink, and Purple Lines—is located four blocks east.We’re a short walk from both Metra’s Ogilvie Transportation Center (500 West Madison Street) and Union Station (210 South Canal Street), which also serves Amtrak. Parking Limited parking is available nearby at the following garages: 191 N. Wacker Drive 212 W. Randolph Street 600 W. Randolph Street Media & Speaking Engagements Our attorneys are widely recognized as thought leaders with deep expertise in emerging legal trends and timely issues. From community groups to conference keynotes, Handler Law attorneys deliver insightful presentations and provide expert commentary. Learn More Careers Handler Law is a growing, nationally respected firm dedicated to attracting talented, experienced professionals who share our commitment to excellence. Explore current opportunities and discover what it’s like to build your career with us. Careers",
